---
/**
 * PageLoadingBar
 *
 * Thin top progress bar driven by Astro's View Transition lifecycle:
 *   astro:before-preparation → 20% → 80% (work in progress)
 *   astro:after-swap         → 100%
 *   astro:page-load          → fade out and reset
 *
 * Rendered inside <body transition:persist> so it survives every
 * navigation. The element is invisible by default (width:0; opacity:0)
 * and the script (page-loading-bar.ts) drives the visual states.
 *
 * On `data-astro-reload` links (the language switcher) the View
 * Transition events don't fire — the browser just reloads — so the bar
 * stays hidden. That's the correct behaviour: a real page reload already
 * shows the browser's own loading indicator.
 */
---

<div class="page-loading-bar" aria-hidden="true"></div>

<style>
	.page-loading-bar {
		position: fixed;
		top: 0;
		left: 0;
		height: 3px;
		width: 0;
		background-color: var(--color-primary);
		z-index: 10000;
		pointer-events: none;
		opacity: 0;
		/* Slower width ramp so the bar is actually perceptible during the
		   ~150–400 ms View Transition window. With the original 0.3s ramp
		   the bar barely had time to leave the 20% point before being
		   bumped to 100% — invisible to the eye. */
		transition:
			width 0.5s ease,
			opacity 0.2s ease;
	}

	.page-loading-bar.is-loading {
		opacity: 1;
	}

	.page-loading-bar.is-complete {
		width: 100% !important;
		opacity: 0;
		/* Final width snap is fast (so 100% is reached) but the fade-out
		   is delayed past the snap so the eye can register the full bar
		   before it disappears. */
		transition:
			width 0.2s ease,
			opacity 0.3s ease 0.3s;
	}

	@media (prefers-reduced-motion: reduce) {
		.page-loading-bar {
			display: none;
		}
	}
</style>
